# Line endings and diff drivers for the whole tree.
#
# Every path in this repository is stored with LF endings (git ls-files --eol
# reports i/lf for all 600 tracked files), but a checkout on Windows with
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF in the working tree. Go tooling does
# not accept that: `gofmt -l` reports such a file as unformatted and its diff is
# a whole-file rewrite, which is 445 of 516 Go files here. CI never saw it
# because CI checks out on Linux.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalise any text file to LF on check-in, and
# `eol=lf` pins the checkout too, so a Windows working tree matches what the
# index and gofmt both expect. The attribute overrides core.autocrlf, so no
# per-developer git config is needed to keep the tree consistent.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell, systemd, nftables and Caddy units run on the deployment host. A CRLF
# in these breaks the shebang line and the parser, so they are pinned rather
# than left to the heuristic above.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.nft     text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf

# Go, web and doc sources: pinned so the heuristic above never has to guess.
*.go   text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.cjs  text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf

# Generated lockfiles and the module manifest: normalising these would make
# `go mod tidy -diff` and the audit job disagree with the tree.
go.mod -text
go.sum -text
